# server + core: a long prompt's preparation no longer runs rank 0 out of memory. A fix to the base engine (no switch
# of the port's, no model code). chat.prepare renders and tokenizes a conversation twice, its prompt and its history,
# and the tokenizer's and the template's scratch arenas were backed by the request's arena, so nothing they freed went
# back until the request ended: ~160 bytes a prompt byte for each tokenization and ~28 for each render (a byte-level
# BPE with DeepSeek-V4's pre-tokenizer, 4 MB of text). On the pair a resumed ~948K-token conversation held ~1.3-1.5 GB
# on rank 0 through its whole reply, and eight such resumes at once took rank 0's MemAvailable from 6 GiB to 0.16 GiB:
# the memory guard killed it.
# - core tokenizer and template: encode and decode take their scratch from the page allocator, render's scratch arena
#   too; only the ids and the text come from the caller's allocator. Pre-tokenization walks depth first, each step's
#   parts of one piece in that step's own arena (reset for the next piece) and BPE's scratch reset for each word:
#   ~6 bytes a text byte of transient scratch instead of ~117, the same ids (a test compares the two walks on a long
#   text with numbers, CJK runs, added tokens and invalid UTF-8; another checks that a request's arena keeps only the
#   ids).
# - server/prepare_gate.zig: each prepare reserves 24 bytes a text byte; while others are in flight, a prepare waits
#   if MemAvailable less their reservations would fall under the floor with its own. A prepare with none in flight
#   always goes, so the gate never deadlocks and only turns concurrent prepares into queued ones; a waiting prepare
#   whose client leaves is cancelled. Without /proc/meminfo it lets everything through.
# - a kept-alive connection's request arena keeps at most 1 MiB and is reset as soon as its request is answered (it
#   held its largest request's memory until the connection closed).
# Switch: TENSORFOLD_PREPARE_FLOOR_GIB, the MemAvailable a prepare leaves while others are in flight (default 3 GiB;
# 0 turns the gate off; start.sh passes TENSORFOLD_* to both ranks). Replies are unchanged: zig build test-golden
# parity 312/319 equal to the frozen Python answers, 0 unexpected differences, as before the fix.
# Applies after the port's patches: this recipe's 0011 export or the per-feature series 0011-0022, the same files and
# bytes either way (engine 0d6850f and 3959dc0). Also offered alone, as a separate pull request on Mia's main
# (0011-server-prepare-memory.patch, after 0010); whichever lands first, the other copy goes as a duplicate.
# By Robert Wojciechowski (GitHub @wojo, X @rwojo). Apache-2.0.
